Here is the complete example.
```javascript
class Counter {
  #value;
  constructor(initial) { this.#value = initial; }
  add(amount) { this.#value += amount; return this.#value; }
  read() { return this.#value; }
  static zero() { return new Counter(0); }
}
const first = Counter.zero();
const second = Counter.zero();
console.log(first.add(3));
console.log(second.read());
console.log(typeof first.zero);
```
Output:
```text
3
0
undefined
```
Only the first counter changes. `first.zero` is undefined because `zero` belongs to the class. This exercise supplies finite numbers and does not add input checks.

A public property named `"#value"` is separate from the private field.
```javascript
class Counter {
  #value;
  constructor(initial) { this.#value = initial; }
  add(amount) { this.#value += amount; return this.#value; }
  read() { return this.#value; }
  static zero() { return new Counter(0); }
}
const c = Counter.zero();
c["#value"] = 9;
console.log(c.read(), c["#value"]);
```
Output:
```text
0 9
```
The public property holds 9, but the private counter remains zero. Complete `add(amount)` by changing the private field and returning its new value.
